{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-112328" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use Pascal's triangle to determine the value of $(\\frac{2}{1}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980995/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5185697440d3e9b5857af13fed04c485e119c1add725cb8db7e35a883b337de"} {"question" "q-112328" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the value of $(\\frac{2}{1}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $2$ and position $1$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe value located in row $2$ and position $1$ is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(\\frac{2}{1})=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980995/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5185697440d3e9b5857af13fed04c485e119c1add725cb8db7e35a883b337de"} {"question" "q-112328" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980995/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0e0184ee9fb99cf69b6b5a911cab20cbe1339b88801e66c5a1a5e76334cd8ab5"} {"question" "q-112328" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980995/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5185697440d3e9b5857af13fed04c485e119c1add725cb8db7e35a883b337de"} {"question" "q-112328" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980995/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5185697440d3e9b5857af13fed04c485e119c1add725cb8db7e35a883b337de"} {"question" "q-112328" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980995/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5185697440d3e9b5857af13fed04c485e119c1add725cb8db7e35a883b337de"} {"question" "q-112325" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use Pascal's triangle to determine the value of $(\\frac{3}{3}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980995/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5185697440d3e9b5857af13fed04c485e119c1add725cb8db7e35a883b337de"} {"question" "q-112325" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the value of $(\\frac{3}{3}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $3$ and position $3$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe value located in row $3$ and position $3$ is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(\\frac{3}{3})=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980995/state.json" "fi