& 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(\\frac{3}{1})=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980995/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5185697440d3e9b5857af13fed04c485e119c1add725cb8db7e35a883b337de"} {"question" "q-69663" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980995/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0e0184ee9fb99cf69b6b5a911cab20cbe1339b88801e66c5a1a5e76334cd8ab5"} {"question" "q-69663" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980995/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5185697440d3e9b5857af13fed04c485e119c1add725cb8db7e35a883b337de"} {"question" "q-69663" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980995/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5185697440d3e9b5857af13fed04c485e119c1add725cb8db7e35a883b337de"} {"question" "q-69663" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980995/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5185697440d3e9b5857af13fed04c485e119c1add725cb8db7e35a883b337de"} {"question" "q-69725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which expression is missing in the following equation?\n\n$$\n(\\frac{5}{2})=(\\frac{4}{1})+[]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980995/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5185697440d3e9b5857af13fed04c485e119c1add725cb8db7e35a883b337de"} {"question" "q-69725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $(\\frac{5}{2}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $5$ and position $2$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $(\\frac{5}{2})$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{0}{0}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{1}{0}) &  & (\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{2}{0}) &  & (\\frac{2}{1}) &  & (\\frac{2}{2}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{3}{0}) &  & (\\frac{3}{1}) &  & (\\frac{3}{2}) &  & (\\frac{3}{3}) \\\\  &  &  &  & (\\frac{4}{0}) &  & (\\frac{4}{1}) &  & (\\frac{4}{2}) &  & (\\frac{4}{3}) &  & (\\frac{4}{4}) \\\\  &  &  & (\\frac{5}{0}) &  & (\\frac{5}{1}) &  & (\\frac{5}{2}) &  & (\\frac{5}{3}) &  & (\\frac{5}{4}) &  & (\\frac{5}{5}) \\\\  &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(\\frac{5}{2})$ is the sum of the two closest numbers located in the previous row. So,\n\n$$\n(\\frac{5}{2})=(\\frac{4}{1})+(\\frac{4}{2})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $(\\frac{4}{2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980995/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5185697440d3e9b5857af13fed04c485e119c1add725cb8db7e35a883b337de"} {"question" "q-69725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980995/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0e0184ee9fb99cf69b6b5a911cab20cbe1339b88801e66c5a1a5e76334cd8ab5"} {"question" "q-69725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :a